{
  "dom_id": "questionExplanation-79381",
  "name": null,
  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "First, we rearrange our equation to isolate the logarithmic term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{log}_{5}⁡(x+2) & =6 \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡(x+2) & =\\frac{6}{3} \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡(x+3) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo remove the logarithm, we exponentiate both sides of the equation. The base of the logarithm is $5,$ so we use this base for the exponentiation as well:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{5}⁡(x+2) & =2 \\\\ {5}^{{log}_{5}⁡(x+2)} & ={5}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since exponentiation is the inverse function of the logarithm, we get ${5}^{{log}_{5}⁡(x+2)}=x+2.$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & ={5}^{2} \\\\ x+2 & =25 \\\\ x & =23.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [],
  "problem_field_keys": [],
  "answer_fields_complete": null,
  "result": null,
  "proof_feedback": null,
